When I was 17 I started applying to colleges. 
What a simple task of picking out of almost 4000 universities and about 2000 majors. 
At the time, I imagined that everyone else had clear dreams of their futures. 
Maybe if I were more aware, I would have noticed the clues. 
That ahHAH moment in debate club - I’m meant to be a lawyer! 
Or that CLICK feeling after I published my first novel at age 16 - DUH, I was born to be an author. 
None of that happened. 
I liked prancing around the town with my friends, smoking weed and giggling. 
My heart felt fullest when we roadtripped out of the state into the mountains and explored the wilderness. 
But nothing that felt genuinely good and real made any sense in the face of the big career question. 

WHAT DO YOU WANT TO DO WITH THE REST OF YOUR LIFE? 
HOW DO YOU PLAN TO EARN MONEY SO YOU CAN LIVE LIKE A NORMAL HUMAN? 
WHAT PROFESSION ARE YOU GOING TO GAMBLE ON TO HAVE ENOUGH DEMAND, ENOUGH FLEXIBILITY, AND GRANT YOU ENOUGH FEELINGS OF PURPOSE AND EXCITEMENT? 
THIS IS A BIG DECISION. YOU BETTER GET IT PERFECT. 

I had no clue and still don’t. 
I just wanted to be with the people I loved. 
I wanted to have fun together. 
To build a life together. 
A life that felt real and interconnected. 
Not a life where we spend most of our energy and time acting out office scripts with people we don’t care about and don’t care about us. 
I wanted to garden together - to work with the earth alongside my favorite people. 
I wanted to create chain reactions of creativity with my friends. 
To paint and record silly videos. 

And yet I felt compelled to go to college so I wouldn’t be left behind. 
No one else was resisting and searching for a different way, and I was too tired and scared to do it myself. 
I went all the way to Georgia to study Computer Science. 
A field I discovered I hated with my entire gut. 
